    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database lists 973 reports for Nueces County from 1953 to 2026, including 134 hail, 287 thunderstorm wind, 107 tornado, and 256 flash flood reports.</li>
<li>Hail is concentrated in spring: May and April together account for 86 of the county's 134 hail reports.</li>
<li>May is also the peak month for thunderstorm wind, with 110 of 287 reports. The May 8, 2025 windstorm produced estimated gusts of 105 to 115 mph.</li>
<li>Hurricanes and tropical storms shape coastal risk, from Celia in 1970 to Harvey in 2017 and Hanna in 2020.</li>
<li>Counts are individual reports, not storm days. Use them for context, then document your own property.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Corpus Christi is known for its hurricane exposure, but the area's <strong>storm history</strong> is much broader than tropical systems. Spring hail, violent thunderstorm winds, brief tornadoes, flash flooding, and the occasional hard freeze all appear in the public record. Understanding that history helps property owners know what to watch for, what to insure against, and how to document a claim when something does happen.</p>

<p>In this article, Corpus Christi Public Adjuster summarizes what the NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database shows for Nueces County and its neighbors, along with the hurricane history documented by the National Hurricane Center and NOAA's HURDAT list. All counts here are individual reports, not storm days. One storm can generate many reports.</p>

<h2>Nueces County storm reports at a glance</h2>

<p>The NCEI database includes 973 tracked reports for Nueces County (county and NWS forecast-zone entries) from 1953 through 2026. Data for 2026 is partial and preliminary. Here is how those reports break down by type:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Event type</th><th>Nueces County reports</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Thunderstorm wind</td><td>287</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flash flood</td><td>256</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hail</td><td>134</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tornado</td><td>107</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood</td><td>37</td></tr>
<tr><td>High wind</td><td>19</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm surge/tide</td><td>17</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heavy rain</td><td>16</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frost/freeze</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tropical storm</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lightning</td><td>11</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane</td><td>1</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The low hurricane count can be surprising. It reflects how the database records tropical events, often under zone entries and separate types such as Tropical Storm and Storm Surge/Tide, rather than how often hurricanes affect the area. The hurricane history section below gives a fuller picture.</p>

<h2>Hail and wind by month</h2>

<p>Severe thunderstorms are the most frequent source of property damage reports in Nueces County, and they follow a clear seasonal pattern.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Hail reports</th><th>Thunderstorm wind reports</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>January</td><td>1</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>February</td><td>5</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>March</td><td>18</td><td>41</td></tr>
<tr><td>April</td><td>28</td><td>35</td></tr>
<tr><td>May</td><td>58</td><td>110</td></tr>
<tr><td>June</td><td>8</td><td>38</td></tr>
<tr><td>July</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>August</td><td>5</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><td>September</td><td>2</td><td>13</td></tr>
<tr><td>October</td><td>2</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>November</td><td>4</td><td>10</td></tr>
<tr><td>December</td><td>3</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>134</strong></td><td><strong>287</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>May stands out in both columns. It accounts for 58 of 134 hail reports (about 43 percent) and 110 of 287 thunderstorm wind reports (about 38 percent). March through June together hold most of the activity. For roofs, that means spring is the season to watch, and the weeks after a spring storm are the time to inspect. Our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page covers what to look for.</p>

<h2>The largest hail in Nueces County</h2>

<p>Of the 134 hail reports, 88 were 1 inch or larger, 28 were golf ball size (1.75 inches) or larger, and 4 were baseball size (2.75 inches) or larger. The largest reports include:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Size</th><th>Location</th><th>NCEI record</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
