<tr><td>Apr. 24, 2016</td><td>2.75 in</td><td>West of Calallen near CR 75 and FM 624</td><td><a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=632317" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event 632317</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>May 10, 2006</td><td>2.75 in</td><td>Petronila, FM 665 and CR 67</td><td><a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=5512628" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event 5512628</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>May 10, 1959</td><td>2.75 in</td><td>Nueces County</td><td><a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=10119647" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event 10119647</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>May 2, 1993</td><td>2.5 in</td><td>Robstown / Banquete area</td><td><a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=10354311" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event 10354311</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The November 2025 report is a reminder that large hail is not limited to spring. Inside the city, golf ball hail has been reported near Alameda Street and Airline Road (June 4, 2017) and on Chapman Ranch Road before reaching Staples Street (April 22, 2015).</p>

<h2>The May 8, 2025 windstorm</h2>

<p>The strongest thunderstorm wind values in the Nueces County record all come from one event. On May 8, 2025, a line of severe storms crossed the western part of the county, and NWS storm surveys estimated gusts of 105 to 115 mph around Petronila, Driscoll, Palo Alto, and Agua Dulce. The NCEI entries describe:</p>
<ul>
<li>An NWS survey estimate of 115 mph winds near Driscoll (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1247377" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1247377</a>).</li>
<li>Numerous power poles snapped near Petronila, some near the top third, with an estimated 115 mph gust (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1249160" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1249160</a>).</li>
<li>Around 80 power poles snapped near the base at Palo Alto (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1249120" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1249120</a>).</li>
<li>A 30-foot tree uprooted onto a house near Driscoll, damaging the roof and siding (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1247379" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1247379</a>).</li>
<li>Mobile homes lifted or blown off foundations and carport roofs blown off in Petronila (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1247382" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1247382</a>).</li>
</ul>

<p>These are estimated gusts from damage surveys, not instrument readings. Severe wind has continued since: in May 2026, the airport ASOS (KCRP) measured gusts of 66 mph on May 23 and 63 mph on May 27, and an 85 mph gust was estimated in Corpus Christi on May 23 from a snapped tree (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1332528" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1332528</a>). Our <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> page explains how these losses are usually documented.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Tornadoes in Nueces County</h2>

<p>The county has 107 tornado reports in the database. Most recent ones have been brief and weak, often landspouts over farmland near Chapman Ranch, Petronila, and Driscoll, some with an unknown (EFU) rating. A few have reached populated areas:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>May 24, 2015, EF1, Corpus Christi:</strong> touched down near Cunningham Middle School north of Golihar Road, snapping and uprooting trees and damaging a nearby residence (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=581315" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 581315</a>).</li>
<li><strong>May 15, 2015, EF0, south Corpus Christi:</strong> started just south of Yorktown Boulevard, with damage mostly confined to a business on Terrier Street (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=581050" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 581050</a>).</li>
<li><strong>January 9, 2011, EF1:</strong> a tornado that began in Jim Wells County traveled about 21.5 miles northeast into the Agua Dulce area (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=279336" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 279336</a>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Flash flooding</h2>

<p>With 256 flash flood reports, flooding is one of the most common events in the county record. Recent examples include reports on June 15 and 16, 2026, of water about 4 feet deep near Labonte Park and I-37 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1332583" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1332583</a>) and closed roads around Calallen, Robstown, and Chapman Ranch. Flood damage is generally excluded from homeowners and commercial policies and covered by NFIP or private flood policies. See our <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>Freezes</h2>

<p>Hard freezes are rare in Corpus Christi, but they do happen. The NCEI database lists 12 Frost/Freeze reports for Nueces County. The most notable recent event was February 2021, when NOAA climate data for the Corpus Christi airport show daily record lows of 25°F on February 14, 17°F on February 15, and 19°F on February 16. More recently, the airport and Robstown reported temperatures at or below 32°F for at least two hours on January 25 and 26, 2026 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1314273" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 1314273</a>). Freezes matter for property owners mostly because of burst pipes, which tend to show up after the thaw.</p>

<h2>Hurricanes and tropical storms</h2>

<p>The Coastal Bend's hurricane history is documented in National Hurricane Center reports, NOAA's HURDAT list of U.S. landfalling hurricanes, and the NWS Corpus Christi storm history page. Key storms include:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Storm</th><th>Date</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hurricane Beulah</td><td>Sept. 20, 1967</td><td>Category 3 conditions in South Texas; landfall near the mouth of the Rio Grande</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Celia</td><td>Aug. 3, 1970</td><td>HURDAT: Category 4 landfall in the Port Aransas / Aransas Pass area; NWS notes 140 mph winds measured at Corpus Christi International Airport</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Allen</td><td>Aug. 10, 1980</td><td>Category 3 on the lower Texas coast</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Bret</td><td>Aug. 22-23, 1999</td><td>Category 3; crossed central Padre Island between Brownsville and Corpus Christi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Claudette</td><td>July 15, 2003</td><td>On the NWS Corpus Christi major-events list</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Dolly</td><td>July 23, 2008</td><td>Landfall about 80 miles south of Corpus Christi; surge of 2.5 to 3.5 feet in Nueces and Kleberg counties</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Alex / TS Hermine</td><td>2010</td><td>Tropical storm winds and storm tides on the barrier islands</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Harvey</td><td>Aug. 25-26, 2017</td><td>Category 4 landfall about 5 n mi east of Rockport; storm tides of 6 to 8 feet in Port Aransas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Hanna</td><td>July 25, 2020</td><td>Category 1 landfall on Padre Island; surge damaged Bob Hall Pier and parts of Corpus Christi Marina</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Nicholas</td><td>Sept. 2021</td><td>Storm tide of 2.07 feet MHHW at Bob Hall Pier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tropical Storm Harold</td><td>Aug. 22, 2023</td><td>Gusts to 62 mph at Corpus Christi International Airport and 67 mph at Loyola Beach</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Harvey is the most damaging recent example. The NCEI entry for Nueces County notes the most significant damage was in Port Aransas, and the Aransas County entry describes catastrophic damage in Copano Village, Holiday Beach, and Lamar (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=718519" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 718519</a>). Hanna's surge reached 3 to 4.5 feet above ground level on the Nueces County islands (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912743" target="_blank" rel="noopener">event 912743</a>).</p>

<p>Not every recent season has brought a direct hit. None of NHC's 2025 Atlantic reports describe a Texas landfall, and in 2026, Tropical Storm Arthur's watches and warnings ran from Sargent, Texas, to Morgan City, Louisiana, rather than the Coastal Bend. The Atlantic season runs June 1 to November 30, peaking around September 10. Our <a href="/hurricane-season-guide/">hurricane season guide</a> covers preparation, and our <a href="/hurricane-damage-claims-corpus-christi-guide/">hurricane damage claims guide</a> covers what to do after a storm.</p>

